  <data name="TourHeader.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>While you wait, discover Linux</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TourSubtitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A few basics for your first steps, to read while the drive is being prepared.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TourPrevious.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Previous</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TourNext.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Next</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TourWindowsLabel.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>On Windows</value>
  </data>
  <data name="TourLinuxLabel.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>On Linux</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_Position" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>{0} / {1}</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_1_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Try it without changing anything</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_1_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>When you start from the drive, choose "Try" (or "Live"): Linux runs from the drive without changing anything on your PC. Use it to check that Wi-Fi, sound, the screen and the touchpad work before you install anything.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_2_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A familiar desktop</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_2_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>You will find a menu to start applications, a taskbar and windows. The look depends on the desktop environment (GNOME, KDE Plasma, Cinnamon…) and almost everything can be customized.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_2_Windows" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Start menu and taskbar</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_2_Linux" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Applications menu (Windows key, called "Super"), dock or taskbar</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_3_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Installing software</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_3_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>On Linux, you rarely download software from a website. Open the distribution's software center, search for the application and click Install: it is free, checked and updated automatically.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_3_Windows" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Download an .exe file and run the installer</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_3_Linux" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The software center (Software, Discover, Software Manager…): search, then Install</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_4_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Updates</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_4_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A single tool updates the system and all your applications at once. Linux never restarts without asking you, and most updates apply without a restart.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_4_Windows" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Windows Update, plus each application's own updates</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_4_Linux" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The update manager, for everything at once</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_5_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Your files and drives</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_5_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Your documents are in your home folder, with the same subfolders: Documents, Pictures, Downloads… There are no drive letters: USB drives and other disks show up in the sidebar of the file manager.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_5_Windows" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>C:\Users\you, drives C:, D:…</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_5_Linux" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>/home/you (the "Home" folder), disks in the file manager</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_6_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Your usual applications</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_6_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Almost every use has a free equivalent, and many applications also exist natively on Linux: Chrome, Spotify, Discord, Zoom, Steam… Your Word and Excel files open in LibreOffice.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_6_Windows" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Microsoft Office, Edge, Paint, Media Player</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_6_Linux" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>LibreOffice, Firefox (or Chrome), Pinta or GIMP, VLC</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_7_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Games and Windows software</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_7_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Steam runs a large share of Windows games thanks to Proton: check protondb.com before buying. Online games with anti-cheat often do not work. Some Windows programs run with Wine or Bottles, but not all: check the ones you really need before leaving Windows.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_7_Windows" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Games and .exe programs</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_7_Linux" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Steam with Proton for games, Bottles or Wine for some programs</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_8_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The terminal, without fear</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_8_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The terminal is optional: everything can also be done with the mouse. But many guides give commands to copy, so the basics are worth knowing. Open it with Ctrl+Alt+T on most distributions.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_8_Windows" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Command prompt: dir, cd, copy, del</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_8_Linux" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Terminal: ls, cd, cp, rm</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_8_Command" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>pwd            # where am I?
ls             # list the files
cd Documents   # go into a folder
cd ..          # go up one folder</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_9_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Administrator rights</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_9_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>To change the system, Linux asks for your password. In the terminal, you write sudo before the command. When you type the password, nothing is shown, not even stars: that is normal, type it and press Enter. Never run a sudo command found on a site you do not trust.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_9_Windows" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>"Run as administrator"</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_9_Linux" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Your password in a window, or sudo before a command</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_9_Command" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt upgrade   # Ubuntu, Mint, Pop!_OS, Debian…
sudo dnf upgrade                      # Fedora</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_10_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Getting help</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_10_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The Linux community is very active and welcoming. Your distribution's wiki and forum are the best places to start, and Ask Ubuntu or the Arch Wiki answer most questions. Searching for "distribution name + your problem" often gives the solution.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="NavGuide.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Linux guide</value>
  </data>
  <data name="GuideHeader.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Linux guide</value>
  </data>
  <data name="GuideSubtitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>What to prepare before leaving Windows, then the basics to get started on Linux.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="GuideChecklistTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Before leaving Windows</value>
  </data>
  <data name="GuideChecklistSubtitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Tick each step once it is done: your ticks are saved.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Guide_ChecklistProgress" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>{0} of {1} done</value>
  </data>
  <data name="GuideLessonsTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Linux basics</value>
  </data>
  <data name="DoneGuide.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Linux guide</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_Play" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Auto play</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Tour_Pause" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Pause</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_backup_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Back up your files</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_backup_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Copy Documents, Pictures, Desktop, Music, Videos and Downloads to an external drive or to the cloud. Installing Linux can erase the whole disk: without a backup, your files would be lost.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_passwords_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Keep your passwords</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_passwords_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Export the passwords saved in your browser, or sync them with your account (Firefox, Chrome) or a manager such as Bitwarden or KeePassXC. Check that the two-factor authentication of your accounts uses your phone, not this PC.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_licenses_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Write down your license keys</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_licenses_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Note the keys of paid software (Office, photo or professional software…) and of games bought outside platforms such as Steam. The Windows license is usually tied to the PC or to your Microsoft account, should you reinstall it one day.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_software_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Check your essential software</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_software_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>For each program you cannot do without, check that it exists on Linux or has a good equivalent, for instance on alternativeto.net. Some professional software only exists on Windows.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_games_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Check your games</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_games_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Look up your games on protondb.com to know whether they run on Linux. Online games with anti-cheat (some shooters and competitive games) often do not work.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_mail_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Export emails, bookmarks and contacts</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_mail_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>If you use an email program such as Outlook, export your emails and contacts, or check that they are kept on the server (Gmail, Outlook.com…). Also export your browser bookmarks if they are not synced.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_bitlocker_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Keep the BitLocker key aside</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_bitlocker_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>If your disk is encrypted with BitLocker, write down its recovery key (on account.microsoft.com/devices/recoverykey). Windows may ask for it after a change to the boot setup, especially if you keep Windows next to Linux.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_hardware_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Test your hardware in live mode</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_hardware_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Start from the drive without installing and check Wi-Fi, sound, the screen, the webcam, Bluetooth and your printer. If something does not work, look for a fix before installing, or try another distribution.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_dualboot_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>If you keep Windows, prepare dual boot</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_dualboot_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Free some disk space (at least 30 to 50 GB for Linux) and turn off Windows fast startup (Power Options), which can block access to your Windows files from Linux.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_power_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Plug the PC in</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Check_power_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>During the installation, keep a laptop plugged in and connect to the Internet to get updates and drivers.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="NavAdvisor.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Which distribution?</value>
  </data>
  <data name="AdvisorHeader.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Which distribution should you choose?</value>
  </data>
  <data name="AdvisorSubtitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Linux comes in many versions, called "distributions". Answer 6 questions: the application suggests the one that suits you best, explained simply.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_QuestionTitle" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>{0}. {1}</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Q1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Have you used Linux before?</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Q1_A1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Never, it is new to me</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Q1_A2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A little, I have tried it</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Q1_A3" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Yes, I am at ease, terminal included</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Q2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>On which computer will you use it?</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Q2_A1" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A recent or decent PC (4 GB of memory or more)</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Q2_A2" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>An old or modest PC (less than 4 GB of memory)</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_MemoryHint" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>For reference, this PC has {0} GB of memory.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Advisor_Q3" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>What will you mostly do?</value>
